ga5j(){return"Capture all three black pawns. Notice they all sit on the same colour."},
ga5M(){return"Queen"},
ga5L(){return"The queen moves like a rook and a bishop combined: ranks, files and diagonals. It is the strongest piece, so losing it usually loses the game."},
ga5K(){return"Capture all three pawns: one file move, one rank move, one diagonal."},
ga5x(){return"Knight"},
ga5w(){return"The knight moves in an L: two squares one way, then one to the side. It is the only piece that **jumps over** others, so it still moves when boxed in."},
ga5v(){return"Capture all three black pawns."},
ga5G(){return"Pawn"},
ga5F(){return"A pawn moves straight ahead but **captures diagonally** \u2014 the thing beginners mix up most. Its first move may be one or two squares; after that, one."},
ga5E(){return"Capture both black pawns. Remember pawns take diagonally."},
ga5u(){return"King"},
ga5t(){return"The king moves one square in any direction, but **may never step onto a square an enemy piece attacks**. The knight on c3 covers b1 and d1, so the straight path along the bottom rank is closed."},
ga5s(){return"Walk the king to h1."},
ga5o(){return"Castling"},
ga5n(){return"The only move that moves two pieces: the king steps two squares toward a rook, and that rook hops to the other side of it. Allowed only if neither has moved, the squares between are empty, and the king is not in, through, or into check."},
ga5m(){return"Castle \u2014 either side. Tap the king, then the square two files away."},
ga5r(){return"En passant"},
ga5q(){return"The black pawn just went two squares to slip past your pawn. The rule lets you capture it **as if it had moved one** \u2014 but only on the very next move."},
ga5p(){return"Capture the d5 pawn by playing your e5 pawn to d6."},
ga5J(){return"Promotion"},
ga5I(){return"A pawn reaching the last rank becomes another piece \u2014 queen, rook, bishop or knight, your choice. It is nearly always a queen, which is why a pawn that gets home is worth a queen."},
ga5H(){return"Push the pawn to the eighth rank and pick a piece."},
ga5A(){return"Checkmate or stalemate"},
ga5z(){return"Checkmate is a king **in check** with no way out \u2014 a win. Stalemate is a king **not in check** with no legal move at all \u2014 a draw. Stalemating while completely winning throws the whole game away."},
