gQl(){return"An open file is a rook's road"},
gQj(){return"A file with no pawns left on it is an **open file**. A rook there sees from one end of the board to the other; a rook stuck behind its own pawn sees the back of a pawn."},
gQk(){return"Move the rook to the open file."},
gQm(){return"The d-file has no pawns from either side. The rook there becomes the strongest piece on the board, and it cost nothing."},
gQp(){return"Where a knight belongs"},
gQn(){return"A knight is strongest deep in enemy territory on a square **no pawn can chase it from**, defended by one of your own pawns. That square is an outpost, and the knight sitting on it can be worth a rook."},
gQo(){return"Put the knight on the square the c4 pawn defends."},
gQq(){return"Black's c- and e-pawns are gone, so no pawn can ever kick the knight off d5. The c4 pawn defends it, so taking the knight there loses material."},
gQD(){return"Rook to the seventh"},
gQB(){return"The seventh rank is where the enemy pawns still sit. A rook that reaches it eats pawns and pins the king to the back rank \u2014 the old name for it is a *pig on the seventh*, because it eats everything."},
gQC(){return"Bring the rook to the seventh rank."},
gQE(){return"From d7 the rook hits b7 and f7 at once and the king cannot defend both. This is why open files matter: an open file is the road to the seventh."},
gQt(){return"Passed pawns must be pushed"},
gQr(){return"A **passed** pawn has no enemy pawn in front of it or able to capture it. It is the one pawn that can walk to the end and become a queen \u2014 but only if you push it."},
gQs(){return"Push the passed pawn one square."},
gQu(){return"Every push is one square closer to queening and forces the enemy king to run back. A passed pawn standing still is just a pawn; a passed pawn moving is a future queen."},
gQ8(){return"In the endgame the king fights"},
gQ6(){return"All game long the king hides. But once the queens and rooks are gone nobody can deliver mate any more \u2014 now the king is a strong piece, and the side who centralises it first usually wins."},
gQ7(){return"March the king towards the centre."},
gQ9(){return"A king in the corner touches three squares; a king in the centre touches eight. In pawn endings those squares decide who stops whose pawn."},
gQH(){return"Ahead in material? Trade"},
gQF(){return"When you are ahead, every equal trade makes the advantage **clearer**: a rook up with ten pieces each is hard to feel, but a rook up with only kings left is simply winning."},
gQG(){return"Trade queens."},
gQI(){return"After the trade you have a rook and your opponent has nothing. The reverse rule matters just as much: when you are behind, avoid trades and keep pieces on."},
gQ4(){return"Recapture towards the centre"},
gQ2(){return"When two pieces can recapture, usually take with the one that moves your **pawn structure towards the centre**. Central pawns control more squares and open lines for your pieces."},
gQ3(){return"Take the knight with the better pawn."},
gQ5(){return"Taking with the b-pawn opens the b-file for a rook and brings the pawn to c \u2014 closer to the centre. Taking with the d-pawn sends a pawn to the edge, and edge pawns are weaker."},
gQz(){return"Rooks belong behind passed pawns"},
gQx(){return"A rook escorting a passed pawn belongs **behind** it. In front, it blocks the very pawn it is meant to promote."},
gQy(){return"Put the rook behind the passed pawn."},
gQA(){return"From c1 the rook supports the pawn all the way, however far it goes. The rule holds for the other side too: their rook also wants to be behind your pawn, to stop it."},
gQc(){return"A knight on the rim loses half of itself"},
gQa(){return"A knight in the centre reaches **eight** squares; on the rim only four, in the corner only two. The old saying: *a knight on the rim is dim*."},
gQb(){return"Bring the knight inwards."},
gQd(){return"From c4 the knight reaches eight squares instead of four, and travels fast from there. This rule bites hardest in the opening \u2014 it is why knights go to c3 and f3, not a3 and h3."},
gQg(){return"Give your king a window"},
gQe(){return"After castling the king sits behind three untouched pawns. It looks safe, but a rook reaching the back rank is instant mate \u2014 the most annoying way to lose, and everyone loses that way at least once."},
gQf(){return"Push a pawn to give the king an escape square."},
gQh(){return"The king now has a square to step to, so a back-rank check is no longer mate. It costs one move \u2014 spend it in a quiet moment, not when the enemy rook has already arrived."},
gFe(){return"Strategy lessons"},
